import{_ as a,o as t,c as s,ao as r}from"./chunks/framework.Bxa7hYfs.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Architecture & layering","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"implementation/architecture.md","filePath":"implementation/architecture.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"implementation/architecture.md"};function n(l,e,o,d,c,p){return t(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r(`<h1 id="Architecture-and-layering" tabindex="-1">Architecture &amp; layering <a class="header-anchor" href="#Architecture-and-layering" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Architecture &amp; layering {#Architecture-and-layering}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>VTK.jl has three layers. Knowing where a piece lives explains most design decisions — and most error messages.</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ Julian layer (src/*.jl)                                 │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   typed wrappers, iterators, do-the-right-thing helpers │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ C++ binding module (wrapper/src/vtk_julia.cpp)          │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   CxxWrap registrations: types, methods, factories      │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ VTK 9.7 (VTK_jll artifacts)                             │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│   libvtkCommon*, libvtkFilters*, libvtkIOXML, ...       │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="The-C-module" tabindex="-1">The C++ module <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-C-module" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The C++ module {#The-C-module}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A single hand-written file (<code>wrapper/src/vtk_julia.cpp</code>, ~600 lines) registers with CxxWrap:</p><ul><li><p><strong>The type hierarchy</strong> — every wrapped VTK class is registered with its true C++ base (<code>add_type&lt;vtkUnstructuredGrid&gt;(&quot;vtkUnstructuredGrid&quot;, julia_base_type&lt;vtkPointSet&gt;())</code>), so Julia subtype conversion mirrors VTK up-casts. Concrete AOS arrays (<code>vtkFloatArray</code>, …) really inherit a template in C++; they are registered under <code>vtkDataArray</code> via VTK&#39;s own <code>__VTK_WRAP__</code> fake-superclass trick.</p></li><li><p><strong>Methods</strong> — written as small lambdas taking <em>raw</em> <code>T*</code> pointers.</p></li><li><p><strong>Factories and outputs</strong> — return <code>vtkSmartPointer&lt;T&gt;</code>, registered with CxxWrap&#39;s custom smart-pointer traits, so Julia owns a real reference and finalizers release it (<code>UnRegister</code>) deterministically.</p></li></ul><p>Building it is one step: <code>julia --project=. gen/build_wrapper.jl</code> (CMake against the <code>VTK_jll</code> artifact; no local VTK build needed).</p><h2 id="The-Julian-layer" tabindex="-1">The Julian layer <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-Julian-layer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Julian layer {#The-Julian-layer}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>src/*.jl</code> turns the raw surface into something pleasant:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">file</th><th style="text-align:right;">contents</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>dataarray.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">untyped array helpers: <code>raw_data</code>/<code>data</code> zero-copy views, names, resize</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>typedarray.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>VTKDataArray{T}</code>: typed <code>AbstractMatrix</code> wrapper with cached view + scalar-type discovery</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>dataset.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">grid construction, field-data access, XML I/O</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>cells.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">cell registry/iterators, <code>MeshCell</code> converters, <code>UnstructuredGrid(points, cells)</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>filters.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>update!</code>, implicit functions, the filter functions</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The package exports <strong>nothing</strong> on purpose: all names are used qualified (<code>VTK.read_vtu</code>), which avoids collisions with VTKBase/WriteVTK/ReadVTK in the same session.</p><h2 id="Generated-smart-pointer-overloads" tabindex="-1">Generated smart-pointer overloads <a class="header-anchor" href="#Generated-smart-pointer-overloads" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Generated smart-pointer overloads {#Generated-smart-pointer-overloads}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>C++ methods are registered with raw-pointer arguments. The build reads those registrations and generates <code>src/generated_methods.jl</code>, which adds Julia overloads accepting CxxWrap smart pointers. Each overload converts its VTK arguments via:</p><div class="language-julia vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">julia</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">_cp</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">::</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">Type{T}</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">, x) </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">where</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> {T} </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> CxxPtr{T}</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">(x[])   </span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;"># CxxPtr of static type T</span></span></code></pre></div><p>and calls the original raw-pointer method. Generic base-class shim APIs also get hierarchy-independent fallbacks for classes whose Julia parent chain is incomplete. Thus ordinary code is simply <code>SetInputData(filter, grid)</code> or <code>Update(reader)</code>, with no hand-written cast. The original methods remain available as <code>Update(_cp(vtkAlgorithm, reader))</code> for low-level use.</p><p>Why not register by-value smart-pointer arguments? CxxWrap&#39;s by-value smart-pointer path dispatches on <code>Type{Dereferenced{T}}</code>, and Julia&#39;s <code>Type</code> is <em>invariant</em> — the dispatch is impossible to hit for custom smart pointers. Raw pointers plus generated forwarding overloads sidestep this. This is recorded as design debt §4.2 in <code>STATUS.md</code>.</p><h2 id="Errors" tabindex="-1">Errors <a class="header-anchor" href="#Errors" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Errors {#Errors}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>VTK routes its output window into a buffer at module init. Every high-level call (<code>clip</code>, <code>slice</code>, <code>read_vtu</code>, …) ends in <code>update!</code>, which throws with VTK&#39;s message if anything was reported — so a failing pipeline raises instead of returning half-baked data.</p><h2 id="The-next-layer-experimental" tabindex="-1">The next layer (experimental) <a class="header-anchor" href="#The-next-layer-experimental" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The next layer (experimental) {#The-next-layer-experimental}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>gen/wrapit/</code> drives <a href="https://github.com/grasph/wrapit" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">WrapIt</a> to generate the same C++ surface from VTK headers automatically (63 classes / 1250+ methods for the CFD subset). See <a href="./wrapit">The WrapIt driver</a> and <a href="https://github.com/ffrancesco94/VTK.jl/blob/main/docs/wrapit-spike.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>docs/wrapit-spike.md</code></a>.</p>`,20)])])}const g=a(i,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
